{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":127,"output":8082,"cacheRead":3648,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6911,"totalTokens":11857,"cost":{"input":0.000122682,"output":0.024536952,"cacheRead":0.0007047936,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0253644276}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Let me analyze the bugs:\n\n1. **Global queue**: `let pending = Promise.resolve(); pending = pending.then(...)`. This chains ALL events (across trades) into one global queue. Different trades should be able to progress concurrently. Fix: per-trade queues.\n\n2. **Persistence rejection poisons later work**: When `applyToStore` rejects, the global `pending` chain rejects. Even worse, it chains all trades. After a rejection, the next `.then` is rejected too because `pending` holds the rejected promise. Actually the test `await assert.rejects(projector.project(created()), ...)` then tries `projector.project(created())` again — but `pending` is now a rejected promise, so the next `.then` would also reject. Wait, let me trace.\n\nActually `pending = pending.then(async () => { ... })`. If the inner throws, the returned promise rejects. Then next call: `pending = pending.then(...)`. But `.then` on a rejected promise — the callback is skipped and the rejection propagates. So the next project call would reject too WITHOUT even running reduceEvent. That's the poison.\n\nAlso `states.set` happens BEFORE `applyToStore`. So on rejection, state IS committed. Bug: state commits before persistence succeeds.\n\nLet me rewrite:\n\n- Per-trade queue (Map<tradeId, Promise>).\n- Compute next state, validate, then call applyToStore, and only set states after success.\n- On rejection, the per-trade queue should continue (chain .catch so the next event can proceed).\n\nLet me design carefully.\n\nValidation rules:\n- First accepted event is CREATED at version 1. Later accepted events use exactly current version + 1.\n- Duplicate/stale versions return current snapshot without persisting.\n- Version gap or invalid first event rejects without changing state.\n- CANCELLED is terminal, rejects later new version.\n- State commits only after applyToStore succeeds.\n- CREATED requires positive finite notional and non-empty owner.\n- AMENDED may replace either field but must leave valid complete state.\n- Supported types CREATED, AMENDED, CANCELLED; malformed reject with TypeError.\n- Inputs, callback values, returned states, snapshots must not expose mutable internal state.\n\nLet me think about ordering and concurrency. \"Events for one trade are applied serially in call order. Different trades must be able to make progress concurrently.\"\n\nSo per-trade serialization. Use a Map of tradeId -> Promise.\n\nValidation should happen inside the queue? Consider: project(event) returns a promise. We need to serialize per trade so that version checks use the latest committed state. But validation reads current state. If we validate outside the queue, there's a race. So validation should happen inside the per-trade chain.\n\nBut \"Duplicate or stale versions return the current snapshot without persisting.\" — returning current snapshot. \"A version gap or an invalid first event rejects without changing state.\" — rejects (promise rejects). \"A CANCELLED trade is terminal and rejects a later new version.\" — rejects.\n\n\"malformed events reject with TypeError\" — promise rejects with TypeError.\n\n\"Inputs, callback values, returned states, and snapshots must not expose mutable internal state.\" — copy inputs? We pass event to applyToStore. We should pass a clone. And return copyState.\n\nLet me write the project function:\n\n```js\nconst queues = new Map(); // tradeId -> Promise<copy>\n\nfunction project(event) {\n  const tradeId = event.tradeId;\n  const prev = queues.get(tradeId) || Promise.resolve();\n  const run = prev.then(async () => {\n    const current = states.get(tradeId) || null;\n    const result = tryApply(current, event); // returns either {ok, state} or {err}\n    if (result.err) throw result.err;\n    if (result.noop) return copyState(current);\n    // persist\n    await applyToStore(tradeId, copyState(result.state), structuredClone(event));\n    states.set(tradeId, result.state);\n    return copyState(result.state);\n  });\n  // ensure chain doesn't break on rejection for next event\n  queues.set(tradeId, run.then(() => undefined, () => undefined));\n  return run;\n}\n```\n\nWait — but we need the returned promise to reject for the caller. But the queue chain should not reject. So we split: `run` is the caller's promise (rejects on error), and `queues.set(tradeId, run.then(noop, noop))` keeps the chain alive.\n\nNow validation logic `tryApply`:\n\n```js\nfunction validateEvent(current, event) {\n  const type = event.type;\n  if (type !== \"CREATED\" && type !== \"AMENDED\" && type !== \"CANCELLED\") {\n    throw new TypeError(`unsupported event type: ${type}`);\n  }\n  // version checks\n  const expectedVersion = current ? current.version + 1 : 1;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nLet me detail each case:\n\nCase no current (first event):\n- Must be CREATED with version 1.\n- If version !== 1: reject (version gap / invalid first event).\n- If type !== CREATED: reject (invalid first event).\n- Validate notional positive finite, owner non-empty.\n- next state = {tradeId, version:1, status:\"active\", notional, owner}.\n\nCase current exists:\n- expected = current.version + 1.\n- If event.version === current.version or less (stale/duplicate): return current snapshot without persisting (noop).\n  - \"Duplicate or stale versions return the current snapshot without persisting.\" Duplicate = same version. Stale = older version. So version <= current.version -> noop returning current snapshot.\n- If event.version !== expected (i.e., > expected, a gap): reject without changing state. Version gap.\n  - But what about version === current.version? That's duplicate -> noop. So: if event.version <= current.version -> noop. if event.version > expected -> reject gap. if event.version === expected -> proceed.\n- If current.status === \"cancelled\" (terminal): reject later new version. Even if version is expected+1? \"A CANCELLED trade is terminal and rejects a later new version.\" So after cancel, any new event with new version rejects. But duplicate of cancelled version? Hmm. The order: check terminal first? Let me think: if cancelled and version is duplicate (== cancelled version), that's a duplicate -> noop? But \"rejects a later new version\" — a \"new version\" means version > cancelled.version. So duplicates should still return snapshot. Let me order:\n  1. If version <= current.version -> noop return snapshot (duplicate/stale). This handles duplicate of terminal too.\n  2. If version > current.version: if terminal, reject. Else if version !== expected -> gap reject. Else proceed.\n\nActually wait, if version <= current.version always returns snapshot — but for a gap we need version > expected to reject. And expected = current.version+1. So:\n  - version <= current.version: noop.\n  - version === current.version+1: proceed (or terminal rejects).\n  - version > current.version+1: gap, reject.\n\nFor AMENDED/CANCELLED with current state:\n- AMENDED: may replace either notional or owner. Validate the resulting state: notional positive finite, owner non-empty, and fields present. Merge.\n- CANCELLED: status -> cancelled. Should we validate notional/owner still present? It just sets status. Keep notional/owner from previous.\n\nFor CREATED with current state: That's a duplicate? If version 1 and current exists with version>=1, version 1 <= current.version -> noop. Good.\n\nNow malformed event TypeError: type not supported -> TypeError. Also what about missing payload? For CREATED with no payload.notional -> that's a validation rejection. Is it TypeError? \"CREATED requires positive finite notional and non-empty owner.\" and \"malformed events reject with TypeError\". Hmm. Let me consider: invalid notional (not positive finite) — is that TypeError or Error? The spec separates: \"malformed events reject with TypeError\" for unsupported types. For validation failures like bad notional, it says \"rejects without changing state\" / \"requires positive finite notional\". It doesn't specify TypeError. Let me be safe: validation rejections throw Error (RangeError maybe). Actually \"invalid first event rejects without changing state\" — could be any error. Let me use Error for field validation and TypeError for unsupported type and maybe non-object event.\n\nHmm, but what counts as \"malformed\"? I'll treat unsupported type as TypeError. For field validation (bad notional/owner), I'll throw a RangeError or Error. The test only checks /temporary store failure/ regex; the committed tests don't test field validation errors specifically. So my student tests can decide. Let me just use clear error types.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — \"AMENDED may replace either field but must leave a valid complete state.\" So after amendment, state must have positive finite notional and non-empty owner. If amendment sets notional to invalid, reject without changing state.\n\nLet me also handle: notional must be positive finite number. finite means Number.isFinite. positive means > 0. owner non-empty string.\n\nLet me also consider: should AMENDED require notional/owner to be present in payload, or just whatever is provided? \"may replace either field\" — so payload may contain notional only, owner only, or both. Merge. Then validate result.\n\nNow \"Inputs ... must not expose mutable internal state.\" We pass structuredClone(event) to applyToStore. We return copyState. getSnapshot returns copyState. Good. Also we should not mutate the input event. We don't.\n\nBut also: the event passed in — we should treat it as input and not retain references. We store `result.state` which is a fresh object we built from event.payload values (primitives), so fine. We don't store event itself.\n\nLet me also consider: what if event.payload is mutated by applyToStore? We pass a clone, so fine.\n\nNow, the per-trade queue: queues.get(tradeId) || Promise.resolve(). But we should initialize. Using `|| Promise.resolve()` works.\n\nEdge: the returned promise `run` — when noop returns snapshot, it resolves with copy of current. Good.\n\nNow the rejection handling: `queues.set(tradeId, run.then(() => undefined, () => undefined))`. This ensures the chain doesn't reject. But we also need: a rejection must not poison later work for that trade OR another trade. Since per-trade queues, another trade's queue is independent. Good. And within same trade, the catch ensures next event proceeds. And since we don't set states on failure, the state remains valid.\n\nBut wait: there's a subtle issue. If validation rejects (e.g., version gap), we throw before calling applyToStore and before setting states. Good — state unchanged.\n\nIf applyToStore rejects, we throw after... no, we await applyToStore, and if it rejects, the throw happens before `states.set`. So state unchanged. Good.\n\nLet me also think: \"State commits only after applyToStore succeeds.\" We set states after await. Good.\n\nNow concurrency test: two trades concurrently. With per-trade queues, project(trade-1, v1) and project(trade-2, v1) run concurrently. Good.\n\nLet me also think about the \"serializes concurrent events for one trade\" test: project(created v1), project(amended v2). first = run1, second chains on run1's queue. They serialize. persisted = [1,2]. The test awaits Promise.all([first, second]). first resolves with snapshot v1, second with v2. Good.\n\nBut careful: in my impl, `run = prev.then(...)`. For first event, prev = Promise.resolve(). run1 does reduceEvent (CREATED), states.set after applyToStore. Returns copyState(v1 state). queues.set(trade-1, run1.catch). For second event: prev = run1.catch(noop) (the swallowed version). run2 = prev.then(...). prev resolves after run1 completes. So run2 runs after run1. Good. persisted [1,2].\n\nBut: the `prev` used for the second project call — at the time project() is called synchronously, queues.get returns the swallowed-chain promise from run1. Yes since project is called synchronously after first project. Good.\n\nNow the persistence rejection test: \n- fail=true. project(created). prev=resolve. run1 = reduce CREATED (state computed but NOT set yet), await applyToStore -> throws. states not set. run1 rejects. queues.set(trade-1, run1.catch -> resolves undefined). assert.rejects(run1) — good, rejects with /temporary store failure/. getSnapshot -> null. Good.\n- project(created) again. prev = the swallowed chain (resolved). run2 = reduce CREATED (states.get is null since not set), await applyToStore (fail now false) -> succeeds. states.set. returns copy with version 1. assert.equal version 1. \n\nNow let me write the code.\n\nLet me also reconsider validation for AMENDED when it replaces notional with a non-number. Validate result: notional must be positive finite. If payload.notional is undefined (not provided), we keep previous — fine. But if provided as e.g. \"abc\" or -5, reject.\n\nLet me write a `validateState(state)` that checks notional and owner.\n\nFor CREATED: build state then validate. For AMENDED: merge then validate. For CANCELLED: no need to re-validate notional/owner (they remain from previous valid state). Actually cancellation just sets status; the notional/owner remain valid from before. So skip validation for CANCELLED. But what if CANCELLED payload has something? Spec doesn't mention. Keep it simple: CANCELLED ignores payload, sets status cancelled.\n\nNow, what about the version for CANCELLED — it must be expected version. Yes.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```js\nfunction isPositiveFinite(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(value) && value > 0;\n}\nfunction isNonEmptyOwner(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"string\" && value.length > 0;\n}\nfunction validateStateFields(state) {\n  if (!isPositiveFinite(state.notional)) {\n    throw new RangeError(\"notional must be a positive finite number\");\n  }\n  if (!isNonEmptyOwner(state.owner)) {\n    throw new RangeError(\"owner must be a non-empty string\");\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNow the main reduce with validation:\n\n```js\nfunction applyEvent(current, event) {\n  // returns { state, persisted: true } or { noop: true } \n  const type = event && event.type;\n  if (type !== \"CREATED\" && type !== \"AMENDED\" && type !== \"CANCELLED\") {\n    throw new TypeError(`unsupported event type: ${type}`);\n  }\n  const tradeId = event.tradeId;\n  if (current === null || current === undefined) {\n    // first event must be CREATED at version 1\n    if (type !== \"CREATED\") {\n      throw new Error(`first event for trade ${tradeId} must be CREATED`);\n    }\n    if (event.version !== 1) {\n      throw new Error(`first event for trade ${tradeId} must be at version 1`);\n    }\n    const state = {\n      tradeId,\n      version: 1,\n      status: \"active\",\n      notional: event.payload.notional,\n      owner: event.payload.owner,\n    };\n    validateStateFields(state);\n    return { state };\n  }\n  // existing trade\n  if (event.version <= current.version) {\n    // duplicate or stale: return current snapshot, no persist\n    return { noop: true };\n  }\n  if (event.version !== current.version + 1) {\n    throw new Error(`version gap for trade ${tradeId}`);\n  }\n  if (current.status === \"cancelled\") {\n    throw new Error(`trade ${tradeId} is cancelled (terminal)`);\n  }\n  if (type === \"CREATED\") {\n    // already exists, can't recreate -> but version is current+1, this is a gap-ish. Actually CREATED at version current+1 is invalid.\n    throw new Error(`trade ${tradeId} already exists`);\n  }\n  if (type === \"AMENDED\") {\n    const state = {\n      ...current,\n      ...event.payload,\n      version: event.version,\n    };\n    validateStateFields(state);\n    return { state };\n  }\n  // CANCELLED\n  const state = {\n    ...current,\n    version: event.version,\n    status: \"cancelled\",\n  };\n  return { state };\n}\n```\n\nWait — for AMENDED, `...event.payload` could include `version` or `status` or `tradeId` fields that override. That'd be bad. Should we allow payload to override protected fields? Better to only pick notional and owner. Let me be strict:\n\n```js\nif (type === \"AMENDED\") {\n  const state = { ...current, version: event.version };\n  if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(event.payload, \"notional\")) {\n    state.notional = event.payload.notional;\n  }\n  if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(event.payload, \"owner\")) {\n    state.owner = event.payload.owner;\n  }\n  validateStateFields(state);\n  return { state };\n}\n```\n\nThis prevents payload from clobbering tradeId/status/version. Good — also \"must not expose mutable internal state\" and security. Good.\n\nFor CREATED, build from payload only notional/owner.\n\nNow, \"malformed events reject with TypeError\" — unsupported type. What about event being null/undefined or missing tradeId? Let me handle: if event is null/undefined or not an object, throw TypeError. If tradeId missing... maybe TypeError. But the committed tests pass valid events. I'll add minimal: if type unsupported -> TypeError. For null event, `event && event.type` -> undefined -> TypeError \"unsupported event type: undefined\". Fine.\n\nNow, return snapshot copy for noop. For the error cases, we throw — the project promise rejects.\n\nNow the project function:\n\n```js\nexport function createEventProjector(applyToStore) {\n  if (typeof applyToStore !== \"function\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"applyToStore must be a function\");\n  }\n  const states = new Map();\n  const queues = new Map();\n\n  function project(event) {\n    const tradeId = event && event.tradeId;\n    const prev = queues.get(tradeId) || Promise.resolve();\n    const run = prev.then(async () => {\n      const current = states.get(tradeId) || null;\n      const currentCopy = copyState(current);\n      const result = applyEvent(current, event);\n      if (result.noop) {\n        return currentCopy;\n      }\n      await applyToStore(tradeId, copyState(result.state), structuredClone(event));\n      states.set(tradeId, result.state);\n      return copyState(result.state);\n    });\n    queues.set(tradeId, run.then(() => undefined, () => undefined));\n    return run;\n  }\n\n  function getSnapshot(tradeId) {\n    return copyState(states.get(tradeId));\n  }\n\n  return { project, getSnapshot };\n}\n```\n\nWait: `currentCopy = copyState(current)` for noop return — but current is internal state; returning a copy is correct. But I compute currentCopy before applyEvent which might throw. That's fine, throw propagates.\n\nActually for noop we return currentCopy (copy of the state at the time). Good — \"return the current snapshot without persisting.\"\n\nHmm, but should noop even call applyToStore? No, \"without persisting\". Good, we don't.\n\nNow, there's a concern: the per-trade queue uses `run.then(noop, noop)` to swallow. But `run` is returned to caller. The caller's run rejects appropriately. The swallowed chain resolves. Good.\n\nOne concern: structuredClone(event) — event may have non-cloneable parts? It's a plain object, fine. But if event contains functions? Unlikely. structuredClone throws on functions. To be safe, maybe use a plain copy. But the test uses plain objects. I'll keep structuredClone for event. Actually \"Inputs ... must not expose mutable internal state\" — passing event clone to applyToStore prevents applyToStore from mutating our... we don't store event. But applyToStore could mutate the event object the caller passed if we passed it directly. So clone is safer. Keep structuredClone.\n\nBut what if event has a non-cloneable value? Edge case; tests use plain. Fine.\n\nNow let me also double check the \"Different trades must be able to make progress concurrently\" — with per-trade queues, yes. Let me add a student test verifying that two trades' persistence callbacks interleave (not strictly serialized globally).\n\nLet me also verify the \"serializes concurrent events for one trade\" still works with my queue approach. Yes as traced.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider: the global queue was the bug. Per-trade queues fix concurrency. The rejection poison: fixed by swallowing errors in the chain AND not setting state on failure.\n\nWait, but there's a subtle issue with the swallowing and ordering. Consider: project(v1) [will reject in store], then project(v2) immediately. \n- run1 = prev.then(... reject). queues.set(trade, run1.catch->resolve).\n- project(v2): prev = run1.catch (resolves undefined). run2 = prev.then(applyEvent(current=null? but v1 failed so states.get is null) ...). \n\nBut wait — v2's version is 2, current is null (v1 failed). applyEvent(null, v2): type AMENDED, not CREATED -> throw \"first event must be CREATED\". So run2 rejects. Hmm. Is that correct behavior? The spec says \"A rejection must not poison later work for that trade\". So after a failed v1, a subsequent v1 (CREATED) should work. But a subsequent v2 (AMENDED) when v1 never committed — should reject because there's no current state and first event must be CREATED. That's correct! The test does project(created) again (v1 CREATED), which works.\n\nGood.\n\nNow, what if v1 succeeds, v2 fails (store), then v2 retried? current = v1 state (version 1). project(v2): version 2 = expected. AMENDED. applyToStore fails. states not set (still v1). project(v2) again: current v1, version 2 expected, AMENDED, store succeeds, states v2. Good — not poisoned.\n\nBut careful with ordering: project(v2-fail) then project(v2-retry) — they serialize via queue. v2-fail's run rejects but chain swallows. v2-retry's run sees current = v1 (since v2-fail didn't commit). Good.\n\nNow let me write the student tests (at least 4):\n\n1. Concurrent trades progress in parallel (not globally serialized).\n2. Duplicate version returns current snapshot without persisting.\n3. Version gap rejects without changing state.\n4. Cancelled trade is terminal and rejects later new version.\n5. CREATED with invalid notional rejects without changing state / persisting.\n6. AMENDED with invalid notional rejects and leaves state intact.\n7. applyToStore receives cloned event and state (mutations don't leak).\n8. getSnapshot returns a copy (mutating it doesn't affect internal).\n\nLet me write meaningful ones. I'll do maybe 6-7.\n\nFor concurrent trades test: I want to assert that two trades can both have applyToStore in flight simultaneously. I can use a barrier: count pending applies. Use a deferred promise that applyToStore awaits, so both are pending at once.\n\n```js\ntest(\"different trades progress concurrently\", async () => {\n  let pending = 0;\n  let maxPending = 0;\n  let release;\n  const gate = new Promise((r) => { release = r; });\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async () => {\n    pending++;\n    maxPending = Math.max(maxPending, pending);\n    await gate;\n    pending--;\n  });\n  const a = projector.project(created(\"t-a\"));\n  const b = projector.project(created(\"t-b\"));\n  // let both enter applyToStore\n  await Promise.resolve();\n  await Promise.resolve();\n  release();\n  await Promise.all([a, b]);\n  assert.ok(maxPending >= 2, \"both trades should be persisted concurrently\");\n});\n```\n\nHmm, need to ensure both enter before release. A few microtask ticks. Using setImmediate or setTimeout? No sleeps allowed but Promise ticks are fine. Let me use a small loop of `await Promise.resolve()`. Actually Node's --test, the applyToStore is async; project chains on Promise.resolve().then. Let me just do several `await Promise.resolve()` to flush. To be robust, I could use `setImmediate` but that's a timer (not a sleep, it's just next tick). Actually \"no sleeps\" means no setTimeout delays. setImmediate is fine. But let me keep it simple with multiple awaits.\n\nActually a cleaner approach: use a gate that both applyToStore calls wait on, and assert that both registered before either resolved. I'll count registrations synchronously.\n\nLet me make applyToStore record and wait:\n\n```js\nconst waiting = [];\nconst projector = createEventProjector(async (tradeId) => {\n  await new Promise((r) => waiting.push(r));\n});\nconst a = projector.project(created(\"t-a\"));\nconst b = projector.project(created(\"t-b\"));\n// flush microtasks so both reach applyToStore\nfor (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) await Promise.resolve();\nassert.equal(waiting.length, 2, \"both trades reached the store concurrently\");\nwaiting.forEach((r) => r());\nawait Promise.all([a, b]);\n```\n\nThis robustly checks concurrency. If global queue, only t-a would reach applyToStore (waiting.length 1). Good — this test would FAIL on the original buggy code, demonstrating regression coverage.\n\nLet me verify: original code has global `pending`. project(a) -> pending = resolve.then(...). project(b) -> pending = (a's chain).then(...). So b's applyToStore only runs after a's completes. With a waiting on gate, a never completes, so b never reaches applyToStore. waiting.length=1. Test asserts 2 -> fails on buggy. \n\nNow duplicate version test:\n```js\ntest(\"duplicate version returns current snapshot without persisting\", async () => {\n  const persisted = [];\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async (_id, state) => { persisted.push(state.version); });\n  await projector.project(created()); // v1\n  const snap = await projector.project(created()); // v1 again, duplicate\n  assert.equal(snap.version, 1);\n  assert.deepEqual(persisted, [1]); // not persisted again\n});\n```\n\nVersion gap test:\n```js\ntest(\"version gap rejects without changing state\", async () => {\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async () => {});\n  await projector.project(created()); // v1\n  await assert.rejects(projector.project({tradeId:\"trade-1\", version:5, type: