s with my queue approach. Yes as traced.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider: the global queue was the bug. Per-trade queues fix concurrency. The rejection poison: fixed by swallowing errors in the chain AND not setting state on failure.\n\nWait, but there's a subtle issue with the swallowing and ordering. Consider: project(v1) [will reject in store], then project(v2) immediately. \n- run1 = prev.then(... reject). queues.set(trade, run1.catch->resolve).\n- project(v2): prev = run1.catch (resolves undefined). run2 = prev.then(applyEvent(current=null? but v1 failed so states.get is null) ...). \n\nBut wait — v2's version is 2, current is null (v1 failed). applyEvent(null, v2): type AMENDED, not CREATED -> throw \"first event must be CREATED\". So run2 rejects. Hmm. Is that correct behavior? The spec says \"A rejection must not poison later work for that trade\". So after a failed v1, a subsequent v1 (CREATED) should work. But a subsequent v2 (AMENDED) when v1 never committed — should reject because there's no current state and first event must be CREATED. That's correct! The test does project(created) again (v1 CREATED), which works.\n\nGood.\n\nNow, what if v1 succeeds, v2 fails (store), then v2 retried? current = v1 state (version 1). project(v2): version 2 = expected. AMENDED. applyToStore fails. states not set (still v1). project(v2) again: current v1, version 2 expected, AMENDED, store succeeds, states v2. Good — not poisoned.\n\nBut careful with ordering: project(v2-fail) then project(v2-retry) — they serialize via queue. v2-fail's run rejects but chain swallows. v2-retry's run sees current = v1 (since v2-fail didn't commit). Good.\n\nNow let me write the student tests (at least 4):\n\n1. Concurrent trades progress in parallel (not globally serialized).\n2. Duplicate version returns current snapshot without persisting.\n3. Version gap rejects without changing state.\n4. Cancelled trade is terminal and rejects later new version.\n5. CREATED with invalid notional rejects without changing state / persisting.\n6. AMENDED with invalid notional rejects and leaves state intact.\n7. applyToStore receives cloned event and state (mutations don't leak).\n8. getSnapshot returns a copy (mutating it doesn't affect internal).\n\nLet me write meaningful ones. I'll do maybe 6-7.\n\nFor concurrent trades test: I want to assert that two trades can both have applyToStore in flight simultaneously. I can use a barrier: count pending applies. Use a deferred promise that applyToStore awaits, so both are pending at once.\n\n```js\ntest(\"different trades progress concurrently\", async () => {\n  let pending = 0;\n  let maxPending = 0;\n  let release;\n  const gate = new Promise((r) => { release = r; });\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async () => {\n    pending++;\n    maxPending = Math.max(maxPending, pending);\n    await gate;\n    pending--;\n  });\n  const a = projector.project(created(\"t-a\"));\n  const b = projector.project(created(\"t-b\"));\n  // let both enter applyToStore\n  await Promise.resolve();\n  await Promise.resolve();\n  release();\n  await Promise.all([a, b]);\n  assert.ok(maxPending >= 2, \"both trades should be persisted concurrently\");\n});\n```\n\nHmm, need to ensure both enter before release. A few microtask ticks. Using setImmediate or setTimeout? No sleeps allowed but Promise ticks are fine. Let me use a small loop of `await Promise.resolve()`. Actually Node's --test, the applyToStore is async; project chains on Promise.resolve().then. Let me just do several `await Promise.resolve()` to flush. To be robust, I could use `setImmediate` but that's a timer (not a sleep, it's just next tick). Actually \"no sleeps\" means no setTimeout delays. setImmediate is fine. But let me keep it simple with multiple awaits.\n\nActually a cleaner approach: use a gate that both applyToStore calls wait on, and assert that both registered before either resolved. I'll count registrations synchronously.\n\nLet me make applyToStore record and wait:\n\n```js\nconst waiting = [];\nconst projector = createEventProjector(async (tradeId) => {\n  await new Promise((r) => waiting.push(r));\n});\nconst a = projector.project(created(\"t-a\"));\nconst b = projector.project(created(\"t-b\"));\n// flush microtasks so both reach applyToStore\nfor (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) await Promise.resolve();\nassert.equal(waiting.length, 2, \"both trades reached the store concurrently\");\nwaiting.forEach((r) => r());\nawait Promise.all([a, b]);\n```\n\nThis robustly checks concurrency. If global queue, only t-a would reach applyToStore (waiting.length 1). Good — this test would FAIL on the original buggy code, demonstrating regression coverage.\n\nLet me verify: original code has global `pending`. project(a) -> pending = resolve.then(...). project(b) -> pending = (a's chain).then(...). So b's applyToStore only runs after a's completes. With a waiting on gate, a never completes, so b never reaches applyToStore. waiting.length=1. Test asserts 2 -> fails on buggy. \n\nNow duplicate version test:\n```js\ntest(\"duplicate version returns current snapshot without persisting\", async () => {\n  const persisted = [];\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async (_id, state) => { persisted.push(state.version); });\n  await projector.project(created()); // v1\n  const snap = await projector.project(created()); // v1 again, duplicate\n  assert.equal(snap.version, 1);\n  assert.deepEqual(persisted, [1]); // not persisted again\n});\n```\n\nVersion gap test:\n```js\ntest(\"version gap rejects without changing state\", async () => {\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async () => {});\n  await projector.project(created()); // v1\n  await assert.rejects(projector.project({tradeId:\"trade-1\", version:5, type:\"AMENDED\", payload:{notional:200}}), /version gap/);\n  assert.equal(projector.getSnapshot(\"trade-1\").version, 1);\n});\n```\n\nTerminal cancelled test:\n```js\ntest(\"cancelled trade rejects later new version\", async () => {\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async () => {});\n  await projector.project(created()); // v1\n  await projector.project({tradeId:\"trade-1\", version:2, type:\"CANCELLED\", payload:{}});\n  await assert.rejects(projector.project({tradeId:\"trade-1\", version:3, type:\"AMENDED\", payload:{notional:5}}), /terminal|cancelled/);\n  assert.equal(projector.getSnapshot(\"trade-1\").status, \"cancelled\");\n});\n```\n\nInvalid notional CREATED test:\n```js\ntest(\"CREATED rejects invalid notional without persisting\", async () => {\n  let persisted = 0;\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async () => { persisted++; });\n  await assert.rejects(projector.project({tradeId:\"t\", version:1, type:\"CREATED\", payload:{notional:-5, owner:\"desk\"}}), /notional/);\n  assert.equal(persisted, 0);\n  assert.equal(projector.getSnapshot(\"t\"), null);\n});\n```\n\nAMENDED invalid test:\n```js\ntest(\"AMENDED with invalid notional rejects and leaves state intact\", async () => {\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async () => {});\n  await projector.project(created());\n  await assert.rejects(projector.project({tradeId:\"trade-1\", version:2, type:\"AMENDED\", payload:{notional:0}}), /notional/);\n  assert.equal(projector.getSnapshot(\"trade-1\").notional, 100);\n});\n```\n\nImmutability test:\n```js\ntest(\"snapshots and store arguments are isolated from internal state\", async () => {\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async (_id, state, event) => {\n    state.notional = 999;\n    event.payload.notional = 999;\n  });\n  await projector.project(created());\n  const snap = projector.getSnapshot(\"trade-1\");\n  snap.notional = 999;\n  assert.equal(projector.getSnapshot(\"trade-1\").notional, 100);\n});\n```\n\nWait — applyToStore mutates state and event. We pass copies so internal not affected. Then states.set(tradeId, result.state) — result.state is our internal object, not the copy passed to store. So mutation of the copy doesn't affect internal. Good. And getSnapshot returns a copy; mutating it doesn't affect internal. Good. This test validates isolation.\n\nBut careful: `result.state` for CREATED is a fresh object built from `event.payload.notional` (a number primitive). So even if event.payload.notional is mutated later, state.notional is the original number. Good.\n\nNow, also test unsupported type -> TypeError:\n```js\ntest(\"unsupported event type rejects with TypeError\", async () => {\n  const projector = createEventProjector(async () => {});\n  await assert.rejects(projector.project({tradeId:\"t\", version:1, type:\"BOGUS\", payload:{}}), TypeError);\n});\n```\n\nThat's a lot. Let me pick at least 4 meaningful. I'll include ~7 for good coverage.\n\nNow, the `created` helper in student tests — define locally.\n\nLet me also double check: for the duplicate-version test, `projector.project(created())` second time — current is v1 state (version 1). event.version=1 <= 1 -> noop returns current snapshot. Not persisted. Good. snap.version=1. \n\nBut wait, the second project(created()) — type is CREATED, version 1, current exists. My applyEvent: current exists branch. version 1 <= current.version 1 -> noop. Returns current copy. Good, regardless of type. \n\nNow let me write the files.\n\nLet me also reconsider the noop returning currentCopy when current is null — can that happen? If current null and version <= null.version? No, current null goes to first branch. So noop only when current exists. currentCopy = copyState(current) is non-null. Good.\n\nLet me also handle: what if event.version is not a number? e.g. undefined. For first event: version !== 1 -> throw. For existing: event.version <= current.version — undefined <= number -> false. Then version !== current.version+1 -> throw gap. Fine.\n\nLet me write the final event-projector.mjs.\n\nAlso need to make sure `copyState(null)` returns null. structuredClone(null) = null. Good.\n\nOne more: getSnapshot(tradeId) when not present returns copyState(undefined) = structuredClone(undefined) = undefined. Hmm, returns undefined. The test asserts `assert.equal(projector.getSnapshot(\"trade-1\"), null)`. So getSnapshot must return null for missing, not undefined! copyState(undefined) -> `state ? structuredClone(state) : null` — undefined is falsy -> returns null. Good, copyState handles falsy. But in my applyEvent I use `current === null || current === undefined`. And in project, `states.get(tradeId) || null`. Good.\n\nBut in project I wrote `const current = states.get(tradeId) || null;` and `const currentCopy = copyState(current);`. If missing, current=null, currentCopy=null. Good.\n\nNow write files.\n\nLet me write event-projector.mjs.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"Now I understand the bugs. Let me analyze:\n\n**Bug 1 — Global queue:** `let pending = Promise.resolve()` chains *all* events (across every trade) into one global promise chain, so different trades can't progress concurrently.\n\n**Bug 2 — Rejection poison:** (a) `states.set` happens *before* `applyToStore` resolves, so a store failure still commits state. (b) A rejection makes `pending` a rejected promise; the next `pending.then(...)` skips its callback and propagates the rejection, so later work for that trade (and, via the global queue, other trades) never runs.\n\nLet me rewrite the projector:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-3cc14ac1-6218-42aa-9ccf-f99e4c23f3f8","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/event-projector.mjs","content":"function copyState(state) {\n  return state ? structuredClone(state) : null;\n}\n\nfunction isPositiveFinite(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(value) && value > 0;\n}\n\nfunction isNonEmptyOwner(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"string\" && value.length > 0;\n}\n\nfunction validateStateFields(state) {\n  if (!isPositiveFinite(state.notional)) {\n    throw new RangeError(\"notional must be a positive finite number\");\n  }\n  if (!isNonEmptyOwner(state.owner)) {\n    throw new RangeError(\"owner must be a non-empty string\");\n  }\n}\n\nfunction pickAmendFields(target, payload) {\n  if (payload && Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(payload, \"notional\")) {\n    target.notional = payload.notional;\n  }\n  if (payload && Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(payload, \"owner\")) {\n    target.owner = payload.owner;\n  }\n}\n\n// Compute the next state for an event against the current state.\n// Returns { state } when the event should be persisted, { noop: true } when\n// the event is a duplicate/stale version that should be ignored, or throws\n// when the event is invalid (without mutating any visible state).\nfunction applyEvent(current, event) {\n  const type = event && event.type;\n\n  if (type !== \"CREATED\" && type !== \"AMENDED\" && type !== \"CANCELLED\") {\n    throw new TypeError(`unsupported event type: ${type}`);\n  }\n\n  const tradeId = event.tradeId;\n\n  if (current === null || current === undefined) {\n    if (type !== \"CREATED\") {\n      throw new Error(`first event for trade ${tradeId} must be CREATED`);\n    }\n    if (event.version !== 1) {\n      throw new Error(`first event for trade ${tradeId} must be at version 1`);\n    }\n    const state = {\n      tradeId,\n      version: 1,\n      status: \"active\",\n      notional: event.payload.notional,\n      owner: event.payload.owner,\n    };\n    validateStateFields(state);\n    return { state };\n  }\n\n  // An event for an existing trade.\n\n  // Duplicate or stale version: return the current snapshot without persisting.\n  if (event.version <= current.version) {\n    return { noop: true };\n  }\n\n  // A version gap: reject without changing state.\n  if (event.version !== current.version + 1) {\n    throw new Error(`version gap for trade ${tradeId}`);\n  }\n\n  // A cancelled trade is terminal and rejects any later new version.\n  if (current.status === \"cancelled\") {\n    throw new Error(`trade ${tradeId} is cancelled (terminal)`);\n  }\n\n  if (type === \"CREATED\") {\n    throw new Error(`trade ${tradeId} already exists`);\n  }\n\n  if (type === \"AMENDED\") {\n    const state = { ...current, version: event.version };\n    pickAmendFields(state, event.payload);\n    validateStateFields(state);\n    return { state };\n  }\n\n  // CANCELLED\n  const state = { ...current, version: event.version, status: \"cancelled\" };\n  return { state };\n}\n\nexport function createEventProjector(applyToStore) {\n  if (typeof applyToStore !== \"function\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"applyToStore must be a function\");\n  }\n\n  const states = new Map();\n  // Per-trade serialization chain. Each trade progresses independently so\n  // unrelated trades are not blocked by one another.\n  const queues = new Map();\n\n  function project(event) {\n    const tradeId = event && event.tradeId;\n    const prev = queues.get(tradeId) || Promise.resolve();\n\n    const run = prev.then(async () => {\n      const current = states.get(tradeId) || null;\n      const result = applyEvent(current, event);\n\n      if (result.noop) {\n        return copyState(current);\n      }\n\n      // Persist first. State is only committed once the store accepts it, so a\n      // rejection leaves the trade (and every other trade) untouched.\n      await applyToStore(tradeId, copyState(result.state), structuredClone(event));\n      states.set(tradeId, result.state);\n      return copyState(result.state);\n    });\n\n    // Swallow rejections on the serialization chain so a failure for one event\n    // does not poison subsequent events for the same trade (or, via shared\n    // state, any other trade). The caller still observes `run`'s rejection.\n    queues.set(tradeId, run.then(() => undefined, () => undefined));\n\n    return run;\n  }\n\n  function getSnapshot(tradeId) {\n    return copyState(states.get(tradeId));\n  }\n\n  return { project, getSnapshot };\n}\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":127,"output":8082,"cacheRead":3648,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6911,"totalTokens":11857,"cost":{"input":0.000122682,"output":0.024536952,"cacheRead":0.0007047936,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0253644276}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787545641343,"responseId":"gen-1787545641-bZXcMmv5DIEasycSK3zV","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call-3cc14ac1-6218-42aa-9ccf-f99e4c23f3f8","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 4242 bytes to benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/event-projector.mjs"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787545767318}]}
