sting (0.074875ms)\n✔ AMENDED with an invalid field rejects and leaves prior state intact (0.480541ms)\n✔ an unsupported event type rejects with TypeError (0.055833ms)\n✔ snapshots and store arguments are isolated from internal state (0.074791ms)\nℹ tests 10\nℹ suites 0\nℹ pass 10\nℹ fail 0\nℹ cancelled 0\nℹ skipped 0\nℹ todo 0\nℹ duration_ms 72.307375\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1787545787272},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 10 tests pass. Let me complete INCIDENT.md:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-5911cc37-125f-4361-8ffd-0963ce09028a","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/INCIDENT.md","content":"# Event projector incident\n\nStatus: resolved\n\n## Impact\n\nThe synthetic trade event projector (`createEventProjector`) was unsafe under\ntwo real-world conditions:\n\n1. When `applyToStore` rejected transiently, the projector committed the\n   computed state *before* persistence succeeded and left its single global\n   promise chain in a rejected state. The result was poisoned state for the\n   affected trade and, because the chain was shared, a stall for every other\n   trade too.\n2. All events for every trade were funneled through one global `pending`\n   promise. Unrelated trades could not make progress concurrently: a slow\n   store call for trade A blocked a ready store call for trade B.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`event-projector.mjs` used a single module-level chain:\n\n```js\nlet pending = Promise.resolve();\nasync function project(event) {\n  pending = pending.then(async () => {\n    const next = reduceEvent(states.get(event.tradeId), event);\n    states.set(event.tradeId, next);          // committed before persistence\n    await applyToStore(...);                   // rejection poisons the chain\n    return copyState(next);\n  });\n  return pending;\n}\n```\n\nTwo defects:\n\n- **State committed before persistence.** `states.set(...)` ran before\n  `await applyToStore(...)`, so a store rejection still mutated state, and the\n  rejection propagated into `pending`, making the next `pending.then(...)`\n  skip its callback and re-reject. Later events for the same trade (and every\n  other trade on the shared chain) never executed.\n- **One global queue for all trades.** Every `project` call appended to the\n  same `pending` chain, so events for different trades serialized behind each\n  other instead of only behind events for the same trade.\n\n## Fix\n\nRewrite `createEventProjector` in `event-projector.mjs`:\n\n- Replace the global chain with a per-trade `queues` map (`Map<tradeId,\n  Promise>`). Events for the same trade still serialize in call order, but\n  different trades run independently.\n- Compute the next state, validate it, then `await applyToStore(...)` *first*\n  and only `states.set(...)` after it resolves. A store rejection leaves the\n  trade's state untouched.\n- Swallow rejections on the per-trade serialization chain\n  (`run.then(() => undefined, () => undefined)`) so a failure for one event\n  cannot poison later events for that trade; the caller still observes the\n  rejection on the returned promise.\n- Add explicit validation in a new `applyEvent` helper:\n  - The first event must be `CREATED` at version 1.\n  - Later events must use exactly `current.version + 1`.\n  - Duplicate/stale versions (`version <= current.version`) return the current\n    snapshot without persisting.\n  - Version gaps reject without changing state.\n  - A `CANCELLED` trade is terminal and rejects any later new version.\n  - `CREATED` requires a positive finite `notional` and a non-empty `owner`.\n  - `AMENDED` may replace either field but the resulting state must still be\n    valid and complete; protected fields (`tradeId`, `status`, `version`)\n    cannot be overwritten by the payload.\n  - Unsupported event types reject with `TypeError`.\n- Never expose mutable internal state: snapshots, store arguments, and\n  returned states are `structuredClone` copies, and input events are cloned\n  before being handed to `applyToStore`.\n\n## Regression coverage\n\n`student-tests.mjs` now contains these `node:test` cases (in addition to the\ncommitted tests):\n\n1. **Unrelated trades progress concurrently** — uses a gate inside\n   `applyToStore` to assert both trades are persisted at the same time. This\n   fails against the original global-queue implementation.\n2. **Duplicate or stale version returns the current snapshot without\n   persisting** — verifies `applyToStore` is not called again and the snapshot\n   version is unchanged.\n3. **A version gap rejects without changing state.**\n4. **A cancelled trade is terminal and rejects later new versions.**\n5. **`CREATED` with an invalid notional rejects without persisting** —\n   confirms nothing reaches the store and no snapshot exists.\n6. **`AMENDED` with an invalid field rejects and leaves prior state intact.**\n7. **An unsupported event type rejects with `TypeError`.**\n8. **Snapshots and store arguments are isolated from internal state** —\n   mutating the values handed to `applyToStore` and to the caller must not\n   affect subsequent snapshots.\n\n## Remaining risk\n\n- The projector does not retry transient store failures; callers are expected\n  to re-submit the same event (the state is unchanged, so the same version can\n  be retried safely).\n- `structuredClone` is used for isolation, so event payloads must be\n  cloneable (plain JSON-like values). The synthetic fixture only produces such\n  events; non-cloneable payloads would throw at the clone boundary.\n- Validation treats `owner` as a non-empty string and `notional` as a positive\n  finite number. Any broader type coercion (e.g. numeric strings) is\n  intentionally rejected to keep behavior predictable.\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":374,"output":1152,"cacheRead":13120,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":14646,"cost":{"input":0.000361283999